dad waited a long time to do a movie  musical and I've wanted certainly to put  myself up there and I actually had a  meeting with Tom before it even signed  on to this I said I want to audition  he's like whoa whoa fella settle down  approaching this was I wanted to do it  live all the way through and from the  very beginning I did have an idea of  what I wanted to do like you chart a  performance I kind of looked at within  the song to kind of figure out what's  going on and try to take this amazing  iconic piece of music and translate it  into the language of film and then we  got to the day and of course as an actor  all you want to do is all that  preparation and then let go and then  just and and I heard Sally Field  describe it she said you know skittering  along the surface but all you want to do  is fly and I wanted to fly that day so  bad and I couldn't and I couldn't and I  couldn't and then I had and then Tom  came out too and I said I can't he was  so kind and then he just said it's not  an iconic song it's a howl I loved  singing when I was a kid I sang at  school and then as I started acting in  fact I was in Cameron Mackintosh  production of Oliver when I was about 10  years old I played the fourteenth Oh  chin to the left but I told him this at  the audition but he had no memory no I  hadn't sung for about ten years but that  wonderful thing is I'm sure everyone  here knows with filmmaking is that when  you get apart you get the greatest  professionals in the world to help you  and on this you know that as well as  physical transformations for these guys  I didn't have to go under any of that  but certainly what I had to do was a  what felt like a muscular transformation  in order to be able to have the stamina  to sing these songs take off to take  maybe 20 30 takes at a time and that was  a four five-month period with a guy  called Mark Malin who a lot of it was  funny actually a lot of the vocal  exercises that we did daily would like  something out of The King's Speech they  often weren't weren't sung they were  involved strengthening your tongue  muscle and things at the back of your  tongue muscle in order to be able to  have a sort of marathon like stamina I I  think the lip-synching to playback is  one of the reasons why more people  haven't embraced the movie musical form  you know I mean at its best you forget  it and you go with it at its worst it  simply embarrassing to watch when the  actors are in control the tempo it gives  the power back to the actors to do what  they do best because great acting you  have to be in control of the medium of  time so you know if Eddie needs to take  a moment for an idea or a motion to form  in his eyes before he can sing so it's  like he's thought he's had to thought  before he's singing it actually spent 18  months working on the on the show and  unpacking it finding out all the things  that weren't actually working in the  show it's a fantastic show but a lot of  character and plot plot developments  don't really work their way through so  we went back to the novel to to look at  some of the source material we went  through it in depth we looked at all of  the obviously Victor Hugo the research  but when it came to the day Tom just had  a way of giving a sentence and with the  soliloquy I always thought this is a  tough thing it's right at the beginning  of the piece in a way you never see him  lower you never see a lower point for  the character and structurally normally  you'd get that and somewhere near the  end or on the third act and Tom said to  me guess who I've never really believed  onstage that that convict five minutes  later and screen time or stage time is  the mayor of the town I need to believe  that something is tapped into so  elementally in that song that this power  rises from within that I feel that  anything is possible and right in that  moment it went from being probably the  most daunting song to one of the  greatest opportunities I felt I've ever  had Hugh was so relaxed expressing  himself through song that you never  yearned him to speak and and and that's  actually pivotal if you can find those  actors who are so at home expressing  themselves through song then then then  you don't keep remembering that the  perhaps they could be talking I adore  Russell in Brussels was the I mean just  he was the cast glue he was the one who  kind of came in and we were all a little  overwhelmed and intimidated by what we'd  had to do but also being really nice  about it and really eager and  Russell just came in kind of said right  coming over for dinner  we're gonna talk all this through and we  all just hung out and he got us to all  relax with it and you talked about  relaxing with it well it was a key part  for us to not only be able to be relaxed  with the material but be relaxed with  each other so that way I became hugh  wasn't just comfortable communicating  through song I was comfortable listening  through song and the way we listen to  music is so different than the way we  listened to own to only dialogue I've  done quite a lot of theater in my life  and everyday was like an opening night  and a closing night all at once cuz you  woke up in the morning you look at my  pillow and think when I put my head back  down on you I may never sing bring him  home again and why I wanted to do this  film is at the center of it it faces  head-on  the elephant in the room for all of us  which is the inevitability of our own  decline and death and it and it somehow  manages to say there is a there is a way  of doing it where you can make your  peace with it and you can transcend it  and that way is through love and in that  that line when he says to see to see the  to look on the face of love is to see  the face of God to me that's the center  of a film this is a love story this is  about a woman who who fights to save her  child and will sacrifice her everything  including her body for love this is  about a man who takes care of this child  and will sacrifice everything for love  and the only way to make sense of death  is through love and being loving in that  moment and that's why I wanted to make  the poem
 

